Delirium
Mental Health & Substance Use
Acute fluctuating confusion, Inattention, Altered consciousness

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MHS-019
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe confusion, visual hallucinations, and agitation in an elderly patient.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted sudden onset of profound confusion, inability to recognize family members, and seeing "insects on the walls" over the past 12 hours. Urgent after-hours assessment was absolutely critical to identify and treat the underlying medical cause (e.g., occult infection, metabolic derangement) before irreversible neurological damage or systemic collapse occurred.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Collateral from child: "Dad was perfectly fine yesterday morning, but last night he started talking nonsense, picking at the air, and today he doesn't know who I am."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with an acute onset and fluctuating course of severe cognitive impairment developing over the last 24 hours. The adult child reports the patient has disorganized thinking, rambling speech, and marked inattention or inability to focus on simple questions. There is an altered level of consciousness, fluctuating rapidly between hypervigilance and lethargy. The patient is completely disoriented to time, place, and person. There is profound short-term memory impairment. The patient is experiencing perceptual disturbances, specifically visual hallucinations of insects on the bedding, leading to severe psychomotor agitation. The family notes a complete reversal of the sleep-wake cycle over the past 48 hours.
Functional Status: acutely dependent for all care; high fall risk due to agitation and confusion.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extremely high risk of injury, wandering, and underlying life-threatening physical illness.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 110 | Temp: 38.2 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Frail, flushed, acutely confused, picking at the bedsheets, intermittently drifting into sleep.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals tachycardia, no murmurs. Respiratory exam shows decreased breath sounds at the right lung base with mild crackles. Abdomen soft. Neurological exam shows no gross focal deficits, but patient is unable to follow commands. Mental state examination is severely abnormal, characterized by acute fluctuating confusion, profound inattention, and altered consciousness (delirium).
Environmental Observations: Bedroom is cluttered; patient attempted to pull out a bedside lamp cord thinking it was a snake.
Medication Reconciliation: On multiple antihypertensives; child unsure if doses were missed yesterday.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Delirium - Status: Exacerbated (Likely secondary to acute infection)
Differentials:

Major Neurocognitive Disorder (Dementia) - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary current issue due to the acute, rapid onset over 24 hours, the fluctuating consciousness, and the prominent visual hallucinations, though underlying dementia may be a predisposing factor.

Acute Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of focal neurological deficits (e.g., unilateral weakness, facial droop) and the presence of high fever.

Primary Psychotic Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to the advanced age of onset, the presence of altered consciousness, visual (rather than auditory) hallucinations, and fever.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

This elderly patient presents with a classic acute delirium characterized by acute fluctuating confusion, inattention, and altered consciousness, accompanied by fever and tachycardia. Telephone triage correctly identified a medical emergency. Urgent after-hours assessment was vital because delirium is a syndrome driven by a severe underlying physiological derangement (in this case, likely a lower respiratory tract infection given the basal crackles and fever). Delaying assessment until the next working day carried an extremely high risk of progression to sepsis, respiratory failure, or severe traumatic injury from agitated falls.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiated immediate escalation of care. Arranged urgent ambulance transport to the Emergency Department for comprehensive workup (chest X-ray, blood cultures, urinalysis) and intravenous antibiotics. Expected investigation results: comprehensive metabolic panel and infectious workup identifying the underlying etiology.
Positive Behavioral Change: (Directed at caregiver): Implement "Reorientation and Lighting." Keep the room brightly lit during the day and use a dim nightlight at night. Frequently, gently remind the patient of their location and the time. This environmental modification reduces sensory deprivation and helps anchor the patient, mildly reducing agitation while awaiting medical transfer.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Nil. Hospital admission required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Nil.
Follow-up: Handover provided directly to ED receiving team.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED transfer initiated. If the patient has a seizure, loses consciousness completely, or begins vomiting, call 000 immediately to expedite the ambulance.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Delirium is a symptom of a serious physical illness requiring hospital diagnostics.
Clinical Course: The confusion will resolve as the underlying infection or metabolic issue is treated, though this may take several days to weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Caregiver: Do not attempt to restrain the patient if they become agitated, as this worsens delirium. Keep the room well-lit and calmly reassure them until the ambulance arrives.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your father's brain is temporarily malfunctioning because of an infection somewhere in his body. The hospital will find the infection and treat it, which will clear his confusion.
Actionable Steps: Pack his regular medications to take to the hospital. Ensure the paramedics have your contact details.

